{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git log --oneline -2; git diff HEAD~1 HEAD; nl -ba public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog'","aggregated_output":"bbffe4b4 docs(blog): UCF Part Three -- apply architect review findings 1-10, 12\n22dcbcc5 docs(blog): UCF as parallel transport -- add Part Three on cross-VM transport\ndiff --git a/public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog b/public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog\nindex a3d25dd9..323a07ec 100644\n--- a/public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog\n+++ b/public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog\n@@ -109,7 +109,7 @@\n             \" with encoded entries. No decoder decision depends on program content, exactly as a hygienic resolver never asks whether a symbol \\\"looks like\\\" a macro temporary. The marks are structural, not heuristic.\"]\n            [:li\n             [:strong \"The store slice is isolated, never merged.\"]\n-            \" The resumed task runs in a fresh execution store whose contents are exactly the carried reachable slice, the remapped resource cells, and the resumer's own FFI pair. Nothing of the receiver's participates. A name absent from the slice resolves through primitives and modules exactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to intervene. This is the renaming environment: the transplanted computation sees its own scope and only its own scope.\"]\n+            \" The resumed task runs in a fresh execution store whose contents are exactly the carried reachable slice, with the remapped resource cells and the resumer's own FFI pair held in a private resource table beside it rather than inside it. Nothing of the receiver's participates. A name absent from the slice resolves through primitives and modules exactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to intervene. This is the renaming environment: the transplanted computation sees its own scope and only its own scope.\"]\n            [:li\n             [:strong \"Primitives are bindings, not meanings.\"]\n             \" A primitive travels as \"\n@@ -237,23 +237,25 @@\n            \"Hygienic macro systems never needed the second half because lexical scoping is flat. Expansion happens once, at one place, and the result is a tree with no loops. Execution state in a distributed system does not have that luxury. The same checkpoint can be read from a stream by any number of peers at any later time, and the manifold they live on has nontrivial topology: partitions, retries, crash restarts, redelivery. UCF needed both halves because it transports an object that macro systems never had to move: something that is simultaneously code, environment, store, and the future of a running process, across a space where \\\"the same place\\\" is not a well-defined notion without a ledger to define it.\"]\n           [:h2 \"Part Three: Transport to a Different Machine\"]\n           [:p\n-           \"Everything above moves a continuation between two instances of the same machine on different hosts. The fibers differ in coordinates (tempids, store keys, host representations) but not in kind: every yin.vm holds the canonical CESK and nothing else. The next question is what changes when the destination is not yin.vm at all but a different virtual machine with its own heap, its own values, and its own compiled code. This part records the decisions and, deliberately, the path that led to them, because the first two answers were wrong in instructive ways.\"]\n+           \"Everything above moves a continuation between two instances of the same machine on different hosts. The fibers differ in coordinates (tempids, store keys, host representations) but not in kind: every yin.vm holds the canonical CESK and nothing else. The next question is what changes when the destination is not yin.vm at all but a different virtual machine with its own heap, its own values, and its own compiled code. This part records the decisions and, deliberately, the path that led to them, because the first two answers were wrong in instructive ways. One qualifier governs everything below: these are design decisions, not landed code. What ships today is the nested value grammar of Part One; the tuple form described here is the next specification change.\"]\n           [:h3 \"What the Specification Already Says\"]\n           [:p\n            \"UCF has one sentence on the subject, and it turns out to be the whole rule: \"\n            [:em \"a foreign engine conforms by lifting into and lowering from the canonical configuration, never by extending it.\"]\n-           \" The specification's foreign engine is a WebAssembly lowering, a JIT, a register allocator: an engine that runs yin's own instruction vector in a physical layout of its choosing, and publishes a static safepoint map from its slots back to canonical ones. A genuinely different VM is harder than that. It does not interpret the yin vector; it runs native code compiled from it, keeps intermediate values in registers, and represents a yin integer or string in whatever way its runtime prefers. The sentence still holds. The work is in saying what it costs.\"]\n+           \" The specification's foreign engine is a WebAssembly lowering, a JIT, a register allocator: an engine that runs yin's own instruction vector in a physical layout of its choosing, and publishes a static safepoint map from its slots back to canonical ones. A genuinely different VM adds one thing to that: a runtime and value domain of its own. It may well run compiled code and keep intermediates in registers, as a JIT already does; what is new is that it represents a yin integer, string, or map in whatever way its own runtime prefers, while still owing the canonical instruction semantics. The sentence still holds. The work is in saying what it costs.\"]\n           [:h3 \"The First Wrong Turn: Lift to the AST\"]\n           [:p\n            \"The first proposal was to raise the safepoint one level. A safepoint today is a pair \"\n            [:code \"(segment-hash, pc)\"]\n            \": a content-addressed instruction vector and an index into it. That looked yin-specific, so the proposal was to identify safepoints by Universal AST node instead, treat the AST as the shared structure every VM compiles from, and describe each VM's state space as derived from it through its own compiler. In the geometric language this was a principal bundle whose fiber is the AST, with each VM's state space an associated bundle, and transport induced from the AST level down.\"]\n           [:p\n-           \"It was rejected for a reason that was already in the specification. The semantic VM's instruction vector is an \"\n-           [:em \"isomorphic\"]\n-           \" image of the Universal AST: one positional tuple per instruction, pc as index, nothing added and nothing lost. A pair \"\n+           \"It was rejected for a reason that was already in the specification. The Universal AST is the canonical source code, and the semantic VM's instruction vector is its \"\n+           [:em \"deterministic lowering\"]\n+           \" under a published lowering profile: one positional tuple per instruction, pc as index, operands saturated with the loader's defaults. It is not a pointwise bijection with AST nodes; an application or a conditional lowers to several instructions, lambda bodies are placed out of line, and provenance back to the tree is a separate side table. That is fine, because the safepoint does not need the bijection. The parking transitions are transitions of the \"\n+           [:em \"resolved instruction stream\"]\n+           \" under the execution contract, and a pair \"\n            [:code \"(segment-hash, pc)\"]\n-           \" therefore already names a point in the canonical code, and naming the AST node instead buys nothing except a second coordinate system to keep aligned. The owner's ruling that the canonical code form is the positional vector, not entity-attribute-value, was made for code; it applies unchanged to the safepoints into that code. Cross-VM safepoints stay at the semantic VM level. There is one fiber, the canonical CESK, and a VM is a coordinate chart on it, not a different bundle.\"]\n+           \" names one of them exactly. Naming the AST node instead would mean naming a point that may correspond to several instructions, only some of which park. The owner's ruling that the canonical code form is the positional vector, not entity-attribute-value, was made for code identity and execution order; the safepoints into that code inherit it. Cross-VM safepoints stay at the semantic VM level. There is one fiber, the canonical CESK, and a VM is a coordinate chart on it, not a different bundle.\"]\n           [:h3 \"Narrowing: the Problem Is the Values\"]\n           [:p\n            \"With code identity settled, what is actually not canonical across machines? Control is solved: the segment hash plus the contract stamp fix both which instructions and what they mean. The \"\n@@ -266,9 +268,9 @@\n            [:em \"is\"]\n            \" the stack: a list of frames, each remembering what to do with the next value, with already-evaluated arguments held inside the frames. Linearization flattens the AST into push and call instructions, and the values that the AST-walking machine kept inside frames move onto a flat vector the semantic VM calls \"\n            [:code \"St\"]\n-           \". Frames keep only a stack base to mark their slice of it. \"\n+           \". An ordinary return frame then keeps a stack base marking its slice of that shared vector rather than holding the values itself; an effect frame may additionally snapshot the stack it needs. Operationally, then, \"\n            [:code \"St\"]\n-           \" is the image of the argument-holding half of K under linearization, not an addition to CESK, which is why the semantic VM files it under control rather than store. For transport it adds no new problem; it is simply the place a compiling VM is most likely to have put a value in a register.\"]\n+           \" is where linearization put the work an AST walker holds in its application continuations, not an addition to CESK; the semantic VM files it under control because it is part of what the machine is doing, not part of what it has bound. For transport it adds no new problem; it is simply the place a compiling VM is most likely to have put a value in a register.\"]\n           [:p\n            \"So the cross-VM problem is narrow: normalize the value-holding components, and separately make sure a compiled engine can produce them at a safepoint at all. The second is a deoptimization obligation in the JIT sense, and we return to it. The first is the question of a normal form.\"]\n           [:h3 \"Does CBOR Normalize Them?\"]\n@@ -277,45 +279,47 @@\n           [:p\n            \"It is one of three layers, and the bottom one. CBOR's data model is trees of plain data. The environment, store, and operand stack are not trees of plain data; they are a graph of machine state with identity, with references into the machine, and with references out of it. Each thing in that gap is something no codec can express, and the specification's value grammar is the list of them: a closure points at code and at an environment, so it carries a content hash and an entry pc rather than a tempid; the configuration is a DAG, so sharing is made explicit in a value table rather than duplicated; two cursor references at one position may be independently advanceable, so identity is carried as an emitter-minted cell that is deliberately \"\n            [:em \"not\"]\n-           \" content-addressed; a cycle is refused; a host function becomes a name resolved under a profile; a stream handle becomes a descriptor the exporter must be able to serve; anything else is refused by path. CBOR carries all of these as ordinary maps and knows nothing of what they mean. Above both layers sits a third that neither touches: whether the receiving VM \"\n+           \" content-addressed; a cycle is refused; a host function becomes a name resolved under a profile; a stream handle becomes a descriptor the exporter must be able to serve; anything else is refused by path. CBOR carries all of these as ordinary maps and knows nothing of what they mean, and its agreement across hosts holds for Jing's admitted portable domain, with host-capability refusals at its edge. Above both layers sits a third: whether the receiving VM \"\n            [:em \"computes\"]\n-           \" on the decoded values the way yin does. A sixty-four bit integer that stays exact, a map key equality that agrees, a negated zero that keeps its sign. Format says nothing about operations.\"]\n+           \" on the decoded values the way yin does. A sixty-four bit integer that stays exact, a map key equality that agrees, a negated zero that keeps its sign. Bytes and reference shape do not establish that. The grammar's primitive rule reaches toward it, by checking semantic profiles so that only compatible bindings resolve at both ends; what proves it is behavioral parity, running the same programs to the same safepoints on both engines and comparing.\"]\n           [:h3 \"The Decision: the Transported State Is a Tuple Set\"]\n           [:p\n            \"The value grammar's seven rules were written for a nested wire form, markers inside markers. The observation that changed the design was that if the transported state is represented as \"\n            [:em \"tuples\"]\n            \", most of those rules stop being special cases. In a tuple store a value is an entity, and two tuples that reference the same entity \"\n            [:em \"are\"]\n-           \" sharing; the value table disappears. A cycle is a reference back to an earlier entity; the refusal disappears. If value positions hold only scalars and entity references, with a program's own map literal becoming an entity of its own, then there is no map at any value position and the forgery problem disappears by construction. A closure is an entity with a segment hash, an entry pc, and a reference to its environment entity. And the carried slice and the isolated store become the same object: lowering is loading tuples into a fresh execution store, the operation the code loader already performs on its projection path.\"]\n+           \" sharing; the value table disappears. If value positions hold only scalars and entity references, with a program's own map literal becoming an entity of its own, then there is no map at any value position and the marker-forgery problem disappears by construction, under an explicit schema that fixes entity kinds, valid attributes, and reference completeness. A closure is an entity with a segment hash, an entry pc, and a reference to its environment entity. Cycles are the one rule that does not move: tuples can express a reference back to an earlier entity, but the canonical value domain still excludes cycles, because the reference machine cannot produce one and because an immutable cycle has no well-defined per-entity content address. Cycle refusal stays; admitting cycles would be a separate ruling on semantics, addressing, and lowering, and it is deferred. And lowering becomes loading: the tuple set is read through a validating loader into an isolated task, with its program store, its private resource table, its module stores, and its control and scheduler state reconstructed as distinct components, in the same spirit as the code loader's projection path.\"]\n           [:p\n            \"What tuples do not dissolve is exactly the residue that was always the real content of the grammar. An entity id is a tempid, local to one machine, the same problem as \"\n            [:code \":segment 123\"]\n            \". For an immutable value the entity is content-addressed and deduplication is free. For a cursor cell it must not be, because two cells at one position are distinct, so the id travels as a tempid and is remapped on lowering. This is the specification's separation of content identity from occurrence identity, pushed down to every entity: \"\n            [:strong \"each entity is either content, hash-addressed and shareable, or identity, carried as a tempid and remapped, and the schema says which.\"]\n-           \" References out of the machine are unchanged: a primitive is still a name under a profile, a stream is still a descriptor, a host object is still refused. Totality is unchanged: the lift either produces the full tuple set or refuses naming the entity and attribute. A tuple set needs a canonical ordering before Jing hashes it, which Jing already supplies for datom batches.\"]\n+           \" That is the identity discipline, and it is necessary rather than complete. Aliasing survives only under an allocation rule: every source cell gets one transport identity, every reference to it reuses that identity, independent cells keep distinct identities, and lowering allocates exactly one private cell per identity. Content hashing must respect those identities, so two immutable values wrapping two independent cells at the same position never deduplicate into one. Around the discipline the rest of the grammar stays: a disjoint scalar-or-reference value grammar, schema validation of correctly hashed input, reachability checks, and the authentication of resource references before lift and their re-sealing on lower. Tuples remove marker ambiguity; they do not remove forged capabilities, and the two should not be confused. References out of the machine are unchanged: a primitive is still a name under a profile, a stream is still a descriptor, a host object is still refused. Totality is unchanged: the lift either produces the full tuple set or refuses naming the entity and attribute. And equality needs defining: Jing hashes the bytes of whatever value it is given and sorts map keys and set elements, but it preserves vector order and knows nothing of entity references or tempid naming, so the transport schema must fix a canonical representation of the tuple set and the identity renaming under which two sets count as equal. Jing then supplies deterministic bytes and a hash.\"]\n           [:p\n            \"This is consistent with the ruling against entity-attribute-value for code. Code was fixed as a positional vector because sequence is its content and a vector resolves execution order for free. State has no order problem; its problem is identity and sharing, which tuples with entity references were built for. Frames and the operand stack, which are sequences, stay positional inside the state, exactly as instructions stay positional inside a segment.\"]\n           [:h3 \"The Boundary: Tuples Travel, Execution Is Native\"]\n           [:p\n            \"The last decision fixes what the tuple set is for. It is the \"\n            [:em \"transport form only\"]\n-           \". It exists between a lift and a lower. At rest in the tuple space it is queryable by the ordinary query interpreter, so \\\"which parked continuations close over this stream\\\" is a Datalog question rather than a decoder walk, and that is a real capability. But nothing executes it. On arrival the destination VM lowers the tuple set into whatever representation makes execution native there: yin.vm's image, a Wasm linear memory, a Dart isolate's heap. The hot loop runs on transients and never touches a tuple. This resolves a tension in an earlier design note that imagined the transition function itself as a Datalog query: that reading is the as-of view over the tuple set at rest, not the execution model.\"]\n+           \". It exists between a lift and a lower. At rest in the tuple space it is queryable by the ordinary query interpreter, so \\\"which parked continuations close over this stream\\\" is a Datalog question rather than a decoder walk, and that is a real capability. But nothing executes it. On arrival the destination VM lowers the tuple set into whatever representation makes execution native there: yin.vm's image, a Wasm linear memory, a Dart isolate's heap. The hot loop runs in that native representation and never queries the transport relation; transients are one way to build it, not a requirement. An earlier design note on the CESK machine in the tuple space proposed, speculatively, that the transition function itself be a Datalog query over the index, and later in the same note proposed the persistence-boundary model, where state is flushed only on park, migrate, or checkpoint. This decision supersedes the first proposal and follows the second. The queryable form is the one at rest.\"]\n           [:p\n-           \"In the geometry of Part Two this is the cleanest statement yet. The native representation is the fiber over each point. The tuple set is the point-independent representative that transport actually carries. The connection is the pair of maps between them, and conformance for any VM is exactly:\"]\n+           \"In the geometry of Part Two this is the cleanest statement yet. The native representation is the fiber over each point. The tuple set is the point-independent representative that transport actually carries. The connection is the pair of maps between them. A foreign VM inherits the whole UCF contract as it stands: the envelope, the dependency closure with its pending waits, dynamic reasons, scheduler and fresh-name state, referenced parked records and module stores, profile satisfaction, total refusal of malformed input, and preservation of the next observable step and the effect trace. What it owes \"\n+           [:em \"in addition\"]\n+           \", for its own representation, is three things:\"]\n           [:ul.bulleted\n            [:li\n             [:strong \"Lift.\"]\n             \" At a safepoint, project native state to the tuple set, or refuse naming the entity and attribute. Content entities hash; identity entities carry tempids; outward references become names under a profile, stream descriptors, or refusals.\"]\n            [:li\n             [:strong \"Lower.\"]\n-            \" Load a tuple set into native state through the destination's own loader, into an isolated store, with identity entities remapped to fresh native ids; or answer profile mismatch, or non-portable for this engine.\"]\n+            \" Load a tuple set into native state through the destination's own loader, into an isolated task, with identity entities remapped to fresh native ids; or answer profile mismatch, or non-portable for this engine.\"]\n            [:li\n             [:strong \"Parity.\"]\n-            \" Lower after lift on the reference machine is the identity. Lift on engine B of lower on B of lift on A yields a tuple set equal in content and preserving every alias of the original. Running the corpus to each safepoint on engine B yields the same tuple set the reference machine yields. The third row is the one that catches a VM whose integers overflow or whose \"\n+            \" Two laws, stated separately because they are different kinds of equality. Canonical round trip: lift after lower of a canonical tuple set gives it back, the direction the specification already states for foreign-engine frames, with identity-bearing sets compared under an explicit alias-preserving renaming of ids. Native observational equivalence: lower after lift on one engine need not reproduce the literal native state, since fresh ids and seals differ, but it must preserve the next observable step, the pending waits, the result, and the effect trace. Then the cross-engine row: running the corpus to each safepoint on engine B yields the same canonical tuple set the reference machine yields. That row is the one that catches a VM whose integers overflow or whose \"\n             [:code \"+\"]\n-            \" disagrees; it is the only check on the semantic layer that no format can supply.\"]]\n+            \" disagrees; format cannot supply it, and the profile check only narrows which bindings may be tried.\"]]\n           [:p\n-           \"Two obligations remain outside the value question and should not be folded into it. Reconstruction: a compiling engine must keep enough at every safepoint to rebuild the environment, operand stack, and frames, which is deoptimization metadata in the JIT sense, and it is what makes lift total or partial for that engine. We require full coverage, every canonical safepoint a native safepoint, because a declared subset would turn a value into a partial function of its destination, which is the unhygienic shape again. And effects: a native engine's side effects still go through the fenced writer with occurrence, epoch, and operation id. The custody layer of Part Two does not move at all, because it lives in the tuple space above every engine and never asks which one holds the grant.\"]\n+           \"Two obligations remain outside the value question and should not be folded into it. Reconstruction: a compiling engine must keep enough at every safepoint to rebuild the environment, operand stack, and frames, which is deoptimization metadata in the JIT sense, and it is what makes lift total or partial for that engine. Full coverage, every canonical safepoint a native safepoint with total reconstruction there, is not a new demand: the specification's foreign-engine contract already requires identical canonical safepoint sets, effect-producing calls included. It is the right demand for a compiled backend generated from the canonical vector, and it is scoped to such programs; it does not claim that an arbitrary Dart isolate or Wasm runtime stack is inspectable or resumable, and a destination that cannot meet a required capability refuses, which is hygiene working, not failing. And effects: a native engine's interaction with its host goes through stream effects like any other, and a protected commitment inherits the complete custody contract of Part Two, the fenced writer with occurrence, epoch, and operation id, the enrolled consumer, atomic admission, intent comparison, durable dedup, and replay or fail-stop. Fencing remains opt-in per stream, and fork compositions remain valid. The custody layer does not move at all, because it lives in the tuple space above every engine and never asks which one holds the grant.\"]\n           [:h3 \"Why This Is the Hygiene Move Again\"]\n           [:p\n            \"The path from the first proposal to the decision is the same lesson Part One drew, learned a third time. The AST-level proposal was a renaming scheme: give every reference a second name in a different coordinate system. Hygiene does not work by adding names; it works by making each reference carry the context that gives it meaning, and the canonical vector already does that. The CBOR proposal was the textual macro's mistake: treat the serialized form as the meaning. The tuple decision is syntax objects for state: every entity is a value plus the mark that says whether it is content or identity, and every outward reference is a name plus the profile that binds it. Changing the carrier from nested maps to tuples does not change what must be carried; it only removes the cases where the old carrier had to say the same thing three different ways.\"]\n@@ -325,7 +329,7 @@\n           [:p\n            \"None of this is new; all of it is newly joined. Plan 9 taught that a resource is a name in a namespace and that the namespace is per process, so the same path can mean different files to different processes without conflict. UCF's isolated store is that lesson: the resumed task gets its own namespace, and no ambient binding leaks in. Datomic taught that a fact is immutable and that identity is a value computed over content, so two writers who derive the same fact converge. UCF's \"\n            [:code \":yin.k/id\"]\n-           \" is that lesson, with the one necessary refinement that content identity and occurrence identity must be kept apart. Lisp taught, through forty years of macro pain, that a name is not a meaning until it carries its binding context. UCF's primitive markers, contract stamps, and content hashes are that lesson applied to a runtime instead of a reader. Just-in-time compilers taught that compiled code can be abandoned mid-flight and the interpreter's frame rebuilt from metadata kept at safepoints, and that the reverse, on-stack replacement, enters compiled code at those same points. Part Three's lift and lower are deoptimization and on-stack replacement across a machine boundary, with the tuple set as the interpreter frame they meet at.\"]\n+           \" is that lesson, with the one necessary refinement that content identity and occurrence identity must be kept apart. Lisp taught, through forty years of macro pain, that a name is not a meaning until it carries its binding context. UCF's primitive markers, contract stamps, and content hashes are that lesson applied to a runtime instead of a reader. Just-in-time compilers taught that compiled code can be abandoned mid-flight and the interpreter's frame rebuilt from metadata kept at safepoints, and that the reverse, on-stack replacement, enters compiled code at those same points. Part Three's lift is deoptimization across a machine boundary, and its lower is analogous to on-stack replacement, though it may simply start a fresh native task from the canonical frame rather than enter a running activation; the tuple set is the interpreter frame they meet at.\"]\n           [:p\n            \"And differential geometry taught that moving a thing without distortion is not a property of the thing but of the connection you move it with, and that on a curved space the best connection in the world still leaves holonomy around loops which you must measure rather than wish away. UCF's seventh section is that lesson, written in datoms.\"]\n           [:p\n     1\t#:blog{:title\n     2\t       \"Hygienic Execution State: The Universal Continuation Format as Parallel Transport\",\n     3\t       :date #inst \"2026-10-09T00:00:00.000-00:00\",\n     4\t       :abstract\n     5\t       [:p\n     6\t        \"What is the Universal Continuation Format really doing? Two older ideas answer the question from opposite directions. Hygienic macro systems taught Lisp how to move \"\n     7\t        [:em \"code\"]\n     8\t        \" across lexical boundaries without accidental capture. Differential geometry taught physics how to move a \"\n     9\t        [:em \"vector\"]\n    10\t        \" across a curved manifold without distortion. UCF is both at once: it is hygiene for suspended computation, and it is the connection that parallel-transports a CESK configuration across the distributed spacetime of nodes, runtimes, and checkpoints. The lease is where the curvature shows. A third part extends the transport past the boundary it was designed for: from one yin.vm to a different virtual machine altogether. The extension turned out to be narrow, and the path to seeing that it is narrow is recorded here, wrong turns included.\"],\n    11\t       :content\n    12\t       [:section.blog-article\n    13\t        [:div.section-inner\n    14\t         [:article\n    15\t          :$blog-title\n    16\t          :$blog-date\n    17\t          [:h2 \"The Question\"]\n    18\t          [:p\n    19\t           \"A continuation in yin.vm is a value. A parked configuration is a record \"\n    20\t           [:code \"{segment pc env stack k}\"]\n    21\t           \" plus a reason and some resource ids. There is no handle in it, no closure object, no thread. On its own machine it is complete: the VM can pick it up and keep going.\"]\n    22\t          [:p\n    23\t           \"On any other machine it means nothing. The segment is a tempid minted by one transactor. The stream ids are keys into one VM's store. The environment may hold host functions. Nothing in the record says which primitives it assumed, which opcode table it was compiled against, or which version of \"\n    24\t           [:code \"resolve-var\"]\n    25\t           \" gave its symbols their meaning. The specification puts it bluntly: the record is \"\n    26\t           [:em \"locally complete and globally meaningless\"]\n    27\t           \".\"]\n    28\t          [:p\n    29\t           \"The Universal Continuation Format (UCF) is the lift of that local record into a self-describing value that any conforming engine can lower back into its own registers, together with the discipline that governs who may lower it. That is the mechanical description. This essay is about what the mechanism \"\n    30\t           [:em \"is\"]\n    31\t           \", seen from two older disciplines that solved the same problem for different objects.\"]\n    32\t          [:h2 \"Part One: The Lexical Parallel\"]\n    33\t          [:h3 \"The Capture Problem\"]\n    34\t          [:p\n    35\t           \"Early Lisp macros were textual rewriters with a dangerous freedom. A macro expanded into code, and that code landed inside whatever lexical scope the call site happened to have. If the macro introduced a temporary named \"\n    36\t           [:code \"tmp\"]\n    37\t           \" and the caller already had a \"\n    38\t           [:code \"tmp\"]\n    39\t           \", the two collided. If the macro referred to \"\n    40\t           [:code \"list\"]\n    41\t           \" and the caller had shadowed \"\n    42\t           [:code \"list\"]\n    43\t           \" with a local, the macro silently called the wrong thing. The code fragment carried its text but not its meaning, and meaning was assigned by the host scope it fell into.\"]\n    44\t          [:p\n    45\t           \"This is \"\n    46\t           [:strong \"unhygienic expansion\"]\n    47\t           \": identifiers are bare names, and bare names resolve wherever they land.\"]\n    48\t          [:h3 \"What Hygiene Does\"]\n    49\t          [:p\n    50\t           \"Kohlbecker's algorithm, the Clinger and Rees explicit-renaming systems, and Dybvig's \"\n    51\t           [:code \"syntax-case\"]\n    52\t           \" all converge on one move. A syntax object is not a bare symbol. It is a symbol \"\n    53\t           [:em \"plus its lexical context\"]\n    54\t           \": a set of marks, scopes, or renaming environments that record where the identifier was born. When the fragment is transplanted into a new scope, the resolver consults the carried context, not the ambient one. An identifier introduced by the macro resolves in the macro's definition environment. An identifier supplied by the caller resolves in the caller's. The two cannot be confused because each carries its own origin.\"]\n    55\t          [:p\n    56\t           \"Hygiene is therefore not a restriction on what macros may do. It is a change in what an identifier \"\n    57\t           [:em \"is\"]\n    58\t           \". A name becomes a binding with provenance, and provenance travels with the fragment.\"]\n    59\t          [:h3 \"Unhygienic Continuation Transfer\"]\n    60\t          [:p\n    61\t           \"Now replace the code fragment with a suspended computation, and the lexical boundary with a machine boundary. Every capture failure of the macro world has an exact runtime twin:\"]\n    62\t          [:ul.bulleted\n    63\t           [:li\n    64\t            [:strong \"Identifier capture becomes pointer capture.\"]\n    65\t            \" The record names \"\n    66\t            [:code \":segment 123\"]\n    67\t            \". On the receiving machine, \"\n    68\t            [:code \"123\"]\n    69\t            \" is different code or no code. A continuation that names code by tempid is not a value; it is a pointer into a heap that did not travel.\"]\n    70\t           [:li\n    71\t            [:strong \"Shadowing becomes store pollution.\"]\n    72\t            \" Resolution in yin.vm runs environment, then store, then primitives. If the migrated slice were merged into the receiver's store, a receiver that happened to bind \"\n    73\t            [:code \"+\"]\n    74\t            \" would change what the migrated program computes, with no collision at all. The first UCF draft said in two places that the slice both wins on collision and merges under the local store. That contradiction was the shadowing bug, rediscovered.\"]\n    75\t           [:li\n    76\t            [:strong \"Free variables become unportable handles.\"]\n    77\t            \" A host function in the environment, a live stream handle, the FFI pair: these are references into one process's address space. Carried naively, they are dangling pointers dressed as values.\"]\n    78\t           [:li\n    79\t            [:strong \"Marker forgery becomes data ambiguity.\"]\n    80\t            \" The first draft encoded closures as ordinary maps like \"\n    81\t            [:code \"{:yin.k/primitive '+}\"]\n    82\t            \". But a program may legitimately contain that very map as data. A decoder could not tell the encoder's marker from the program's literal, exactly as an unhygienic expander cannot tell the macro's \"\n    83\t            [:code \"tmp\"]\n    84\t            \" from the caller's.\"]]\n    85\t          [:h3 \"Hygienic Execution State\"]\n    86\t          [:p\n    87\t           \"UCF's answer is the hygiene move, applied to every component of the configuration. Each answer attaches context so that the value resolves against its origin rather than against wherever it lands.\"]\n    88\t          [:ul.bulleted\n    89\t           [:li\n    90\t            [:strong \"Code carries its identity, not its address.\"]\n    91\t            \" Every segment a continuation may reference bears \"\n    92\t            [:code \":yin.code/hash\"]\n    93\t            \", the content address of its canonical instruction vector: one positional tuple per instruction, pc as index, operands saturated with the loader's defaults, provenance and entity ids excluded. Two segments address equally if and only if their loader-resolved interpretations are equal. The name says \"\n    94\t            [:em \"what the code is\"]\n    95\t            \", so it means the same thing on every machine that can hash.\"]\n    96\t           [:li\n    97\t            [:strong \"Semantics carries a stamp.\"]\n    98\t            \" An address fixes which instructions; it does not fix what \"\n    99\t            [:code \":call\"]\n   100\t            \" means. So the value carries \"\n   101\t            [:code \":yin.k/contract\"]\n   102\t            \", naming one published revision of the complete execution contract: opcode table, transitions, resolution precedence, effect outcomes, scheduler semantics. A resumer on a different revision returns \"\n   103\t            [:code \":yin.k/profile-mismatch\"]\n   104\t            \" before lowering. This is the definition-site environment of the macro, made explicit as a version.\"]\n   105\t           [:li\n   106\t            [:strong \"The grammar is disjoint by construction.\"]\n   107\t            \" Every map at an encoded-value position is a UCF marker. A program's own maps are always wrapped as \"\n   108\t            [:code \":yin.k/literal\"]\n   109\t            \" with encoded entries. No decoder decision depends on program content, exactly as a hygienic resolver never asks whether a symbol \\\"looks like\\\" a macro temporary. The marks are structural, not heuristic.\"]\n   110\t           [:li\n   111\t            [:strong \"The store slice is isolated, never merged.\"]\n   112\t            \" The resumed task runs in a fresh execution store whose contents are exactly the carried reachable slice, with the remapped resource cells and the resumer's own FFI pair held in a private resource table beside it rather than inside it. Nothing of the receiver's participates. A name absent from the slice resolves through primitives and modules exactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to intervene. This is the renaming environment: the transplanted computation sees its own scope and only its own scope.\"]\n   113\t           [:li\n   114\t            [:strong \"Primitives are bindings, not meanings.\"]\n   115\t            \" A primitive travels as \"\n   116\t            [:code \"{:yin.k/primitive :yin.k/name sym}\"]\n   117\t            \", and the name is resolved at the resumer under a profile check. A symbol in the environment that referred to a host function is recorded by reverse lookup against the emitter's primitive table, and refused if no portable binding exists. The hygienic system's rule that an identifier is a name plus a binding context is here verbatim.\"]\n   118\t           [:li\n   119\t            [:strong \"Anything else is refused, totally and by path.\"]\n   120\t            \" Encoding is one total function from value and path to outcome. A live handle, a host object, a closure over a non-portable value: each is \"\n   121\t            [:code \":yin.k/non-portable\"]\n   122\t            \" naming the path where it was found. The lift never produces a half-hygienic value.\"]]\n   123\t          [:p\n   124\t           \"The structural resemblance is not a metaphor. A hygienic macro system and UCF are solving the same problem on the same abstract object: a fragment that must mean the same thing after transplantation as it did before. The macro system does it for a fragment of syntax entering a new lexical scope. UCF does it for a fragment of execution entering a new machine. Both work by refusing bare names and insisting that every reference carry the context that gives it meaning.\"]\n   125\t          [:h2 \"Part Two: The Geometric Parallel\"]\n   126\t          [:h3 \"Moving a Vector on a Curved Surface\"]\n   127\t          [:p\n   128\t           \"In differential geometry, a vector lives in the tangent space at a point. It does not exist \\\"everywhere\\\"; it exists \"\n   129\t           [:em \"at\"]\n   130\t           \" a point. To compare a vector at one point with a vector at another, you must transport one of them along a path. On a flat surface this is trivial: slide it and keep it parallel to itself. On a curved surface there is no canonical way to keep a vector \\\"the same\\\" as it moves, because the tangent spaces at different points are different vector spaces with no built-in identification.\"]\n   131\t          [:p\n   132\t           \"The structure that supplies the identification is a \"\n   133\t           [:strong \"connection\"]\n   134\t           \", written \"\n   135\t           [:code \"∇\"]\n   136\t           \". A connection says, for each infinitesimal step along a path, how the tangent space at the new point is identified with the one just left. Parallel transport along a curve \"\n   137\t           [:code \"γ\"]\n   138\t           \" is the rule \"\n   139\t           [:code \"∇_γ̇ v = 0\"]\n   140\t           \": the covariant derivative of the vector along the curve vanishes. The vector changes coordinates as it moves, because the coordinates at each point differ, but its intrinsic content is preserved by the connection.\"]\n   141\t          [:p\n   142\t           \"The general picture is a \"\n   143\t           [:strong \"fiber bundle\"]\n   144\t           \". There is a base manifold \"\n   145\t           [:code \"M\"]\n   146\t           \" of points. Over each point \"\n   147\t           [:code \"x\"]\n   148\t           \" sits a fiber \"\n   149\t           [:code \"F_x\"]\n   150\t           \", a copy of some space of states. The total space is all fibers glued together. A section picks one state per point. A connection says how to carry a state from one fiber to a neighboring fiber. And the deep fact is this: carrying a state around a closed loop and returning to the start does not in general give back the same state. The failure to return is \"\n   151\t           [:strong \"holonomy\"]\n   152\t           \", and its infinitesimal measure is \"\n   153\t           [:strong \"curvature\"]\n   154\t           \". Curvature is the obstruction to a globally consistent notion of \\\"the same state everywhere.\\\"\"]\n   155\t          [:h3 \"The Bundle of Execution\"]\n   156\t          [:p \"Every term of that picture has a referent in datom.world, and the correspondence is exact enough to compute with.\"]\n   157\t          [:ul.bulleted\n   158\t           [:li\n   159\t            [:strong \"Base manifold M: distributed spacetime.\"]\n   160\t            \" The points are places a computation can be: this JVM, that Node.js process, a Dart isolate on a phone, a checkpoint persisted at a particular transaction time. Points are separated both in space (different hosts) and in time (a park at t₁, a resume at t₂). The manifold is not flat: hosts differ in codec, in number representation, in which primitives exist, in which code is already loaded.\"]\n   161\t           [:li\n   162\t            [:strong \"Fiber F_x: the local CESK state space.\"]\n   163\t            \" Over each point sits the space of configurations \"\n   164\t            [:code \"⟨C, E, S, K⟩\"]\n   165\t            \" that machine can hold. Control, environment, store, continuation. These fibers are isomorphic in the abstract (every conforming engine lowers into the canonical CESK), but they are not \"\n   166\t            [:em \"identical\"]\n   167\t            \": each has its own local ids, its own store keys, its own resource table, its own host representations. Exactly as tangent spaces at different points are each ℝⁿ yet have no canonical identification, two VMs each hold CESK configurations yet have no canonical map between their segment ids.\"]\n   168\t           [:li\n   169\t            [:strong \"Path γ: the dao.stream.\"]\n   170\t            \" The curve along which a suspended computation moves is a stream. It may be a file, a socket, a DHT entry, an append log. The specification calls this role the \"\n   171\t            [:em \"carrier\"]\n   172\t            \" and says of it: sees opaque data, holds nothing. That is exactly what a path in the base is. A path does not carry meaning. It carries position.\"]\n   173\t           [:li\n   174\t            [:strong \"Connection ∇: the Universal Continuation Format.\"]\n   175\t            \" UCF is the rule that identifies a configuration in the fiber over the emitter with a configuration in the fiber over the resumer. The lift takes a local configuration to a point-independent representative: code by content hash, primitives by name plus profile, store as an isolated slice, markers in a disjoint grammar. The lower takes that representative back into the resumer's local coordinates: fresh local ids minted, resources remapped into a private table, references re-sealed under the receiver's own capability secret. Content is invariant; coordinates change. That is \"\n   176\t            [:code \"∇_γ̇ v = 0\"]\n   177\t            \" for execution state.\"]]\n   178\t          [:p\n   179\t           \"A connection has one defining property beyond preserving content: it is defined pointwise along the path and depends on nothing global. UCF has the same property, and it is a stated invariant rather than an accident. The reference engine is \"\n   180\t           [:code \"yin.vm.semantic\"]\n   181\t           \", and a foreign engine conforms by lifting into and lowering from its canonical configuration, never by extending it. There is no global coordinate system. There is only the local lift at the emitter, the local lower at the resumer, and the connection that relates them.\"]\n   182\t          [:h3 \"Where the Curvature Is\"]\n   183\t          [:p\n   184\t           \"If the manifold were flat, transport would be the end of the story. Lift, carry, lower, done. The reason UCF has a seventh section, longer than all the others, is that the manifold of distributed execution is curved, and the curvature has a name: \"\n   185\t           [:strong \"the resumption race\"]\n   186\t           \".\"]\n   187\t          [:p\n   188\t           \"The emitter parks, lifts, appends the value to a medium, and keeps its local parked record. Three readers see the value on the stream. The emitter's own wait set wakes because the stream it was waiting on delivered. Now four machines believe they may continue one computation. Nothing in dao.stream can stop them: a stream has no privileged reader and no destructive read.\"]\n   189\t          [:p\n   190\t           \"This is holonomy. Carry the state out along one path and back along another, and you do not get one state, you get several, each claiming to be the continuation of the same computation. Two resumers running the same checkpoint are two parallel-transported copies of one vector that have been brought back to the same point and found to disagree. The geometric content of \\\"split-brain\\\" is exactly that: transport around a loop is not the identity.\"]\n   191\t          [:p\n   192\t           \"UCF does not pretend the manifold is flat. It measures the curvature and fences it:\"]\n   193\t          [:ul.bulleted\n   194\t           [:li\n   195\t            [:strong \"Content is not occurrence.\"]\n   196\t            \" The same parked computation may be encoded more than once with different sharing thresholds, so the encodings have different content addresses while remaining one checkpoint occurrence. \"\n   197\t            [:code \":yin.k/id\"]\n   198\t            \" is content identity, for deduplication and materialization. \"\n   199\t            [:code \":yin.k/occurrence\"]\n   200\t            \" is the thing custody names, minted exactly once when a park is first exported, carried unchanged by every snapshot variant and every retry. In geometric terms: the vector has many coordinate expressions, but one identity in the bundle.\"]\n   201\t           [:li\n   202\t            [:strong \"Resumption is a take, a take is a write, exclusion is a lease.\"]\n   203\t            \" Custody is arbitrated above the stream on an arbitration medium, a dao.space whose facts are ordinary datoms, using the dao.lease vocabulary unchanged. The leased subject is the occurrence. Only grantor-authored facts establish terms. The grantor is the transactor of the occurrence's execution ledger, the one boundary that actually possesses the right to admit effects as this occurrence.\"]\n   204\t           [:li\n   205\t            [:strong \"Epochs are the fence.\"]\n   206\t            \" The ledger carries an epoch datom for each occurrence, advanced by the grantor on every reclaim. A resumed incarnation emits through a fenced writer that stamps each effect with its lease, its epoch, and a stable operation id \"\n   207\t            [:code \"{:yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/seq n}\"]\n   208\t            \". An enrolled consumer checks the epoch, compares the operation's canonical intent against its durable dedup record, and commits the side effect, all in one atomic boundary. A stale epoch is refused at commitment. A lapsed holder is never told to stop; it stops at its own lease bound, and what it did past the bound is distinguishable after the fact by epoch and op-id.\"]\n   209\t           [:li\n   210\t            [:strong \"The absence of a lapse is never evidence of tenure.\"]\n   211\t            \" The first draft tried to fence by having consumers look for \"\n   212\t            [:code \":lapsed\"]\n   213\t            \" records and drop effects whose incarnation had one. That is correlation, not fencing: a partitioned consumer cannot see the lapse, and accepts stale effects. The epoch check at commitment replaces it. In the geometric reading, you cannot determine that two transported vectors agree by checking that nobody has yet reported them disagreeing. You need a gauge, and the epoch is the gauge.\"]\n   214\t           [:li\n   215\t            [:strong \"Causal linearity is a chain of edges.\"]\n   216\t            \" Completion of a checkpoint is not the holder's report; it is the grantor's ledger transition that atomically records the lapse, the occurrence-completed fact, and exactly one successor or terminal edge. A successor published without an accepted report is an orphan, queryable as history but never a grant. The world line of a computation is thereby a single path through occurrences, and the ledger is what makes it single.\"]]\n   217\t          [:p\n   218\t           \"Notice what UCF does \"\n   219\t           [:em \"not\"]\n   220\t           \" do. It does not make the manifold flat by fiat. It does not promise exactly-once effects through a bare stream; a composition that needs them must wire a fenced writer and an enrolled consumer, and one that cannot reach the authority must suspend protected commitment or declare at-least-once. A composition that cannot wire a dao.space transactor as grantor cannot offer exclusive custody at all; it composes fork semantics only. The specification calls this an honest capability limit, not a gap to paper over. Geometry agrees: you cannot remove curvature by choosing better coordinates. You can only measure it and account for it.\"]\n   221\t          [:h2 \"Why the Two Parallels Are One\"]\n   222\t          [:p\n   223\t           \"Hygiene and parallel transport look like different ideas from different fields. They are the same idea seen at two scales.\"]\n   224\t          [:p\n   225\t           \"Hygiene is the \"\n   226\t           [:em \"fiberwise\"]\n   227\t           \" story. It says: a value in a fiber must carry enough context that the lower, in a different fiber, resolves every reference against the origin rather than the destination. Content-addressed code, stamped contracts, isolated stores, disjoint tagged markers, named primitives. These are the marks and scopes of the syntax object, placed on execution state. They make the connection \"\n   228\t           [:strong \"well defined\"]\n   229\t           \": given a lifted value, there is exactly one configuration it lowers to on any conforming engine.\"]\n   230\t          [:p\n   231\t           \"Parallel transport is the \"\n   232\t           [:em \"global\"]\n   233\t           \" story. It says: even a perfectly well-defined connection does not give a globally consistent identification of states when the base is curved, and the failure is measured by holonomy around loops. Occurrence identity, lease custody, epochs, fenced writers, the ledger as authority. These do not make the connection better defined. They make the path \"\n   234\t           [:strong \"single\"]\n   235\t           \": they ensure that of all the loops the value might be carried around, exactly one closes as the continuation of the computation, and every other closure is distinguishable as a lapsed incarnation.\"]\n   236\t          [:p\n   237\t           \"Hygienic macro systems never needed the second half because lexical scoping is flat. Expansion happens once, at one place, and the result is a tree with no loops. Execution state in a distributed system does not have that luxury. The same checkpoint can be read from a stream by any number of peers at any later time, and the manifold they live on has nontrivial topology: partitions, retries, crash restarts, redelivery. UCF needed both halves because it transports an object that macro systems never had to move: something that is simultaneously code, environment, store, and the future of a running process, across a space where \\\"the same place\\\" is not a well-defined notion without a ledger to define it.\"]\n   238\t          [:h2 \"Part Three: Transport to a Different Machine\"]\n   239\t          [:p\n   240\t           \"Everything above moves a continuation between two instances of the same machine on different hosts. The fibers differ in coordinates (tempids, store keys, host representations) but not in kind: every yin.vm holds the canonical CESK and nothing else. The next question is what changes when the destination is not yin.vm at all but a different virtual machine with its own heap, its own values, and its own compiled code. This part records the decisions and, deliberately, the path that led to them, because the first two answers were wrong in instructive ways. One qualifier governs everything below: these are design decisions, not landed code. What ships today is the nested value grammar of Part One; the tuple form described here is the next specification change.\"]\n   241\t          [:h3 \"What the Specification Already Says\"]\n   242\t          [:p\n   243\t           \"UCF has one sentence on the subject, and it turns out to be the whole rule: \"\n   244\t           [:em \"a foreign engine conforms by lifting into and lowering from the canonical configuration, never by extending it.\"]\n   245\t           \" The specification's foreign engine is a WebAssembly lowering, a JIT, a register allocator: an engine that runs yin's own instruction vector in a physical layout of its choosing, and publishes a static safepoint map from its slots back to canonical ones. A genuinely different VM adds one thing to that: a runtime and value domain of its own. It may well run compiled code and keep intermediates in registers, as a JIT already does; what is new is that it represents a yin integer, string, or map in whatever way its own runtime prefers, while still owing the canonical instruction semantics. The sentence still holds. The work is in saying what it costs.\"]\n   246\t          [:h3 \"The First Wrong Turn: Lift to the AST\"]\n   247\t          [:p\n   248\t           \"The first proposal was to raise the safepoint one level. A safepoint today is a pair \"\n   249\t           [:code \"(segment-hash, pc)\"]\n   250\t           \": a content-addressed instruction vector and an index into it. That looked yin-specific, so the proposal was to identify safepoints by Universal AST node instead, treat the AST as the shared structure every VM compiles from, and describe each VM's state space as derived from it through its own compiler. In the geometric language this was a principal bundle whose fiber is the AST, with each VM's state space an associated bundle, and transport induced from the AST level down.\"]\n   251\t          [:p\n   252\t           \"It was rejected for a reason that was already in the specification. The Universal AST is the canonical source code, and the semantic VM's instruction vector is its \"\n   253\t           [:em \"deterministic lowering\"]\n   254\t           \" under a published lowering profile: one positional tuple per instruction, pc as index, operands saturated with the loader's defaults. It is not a pointwise bijection with AST nodes; an application or a conditional lowers to several instructions, lambda bodies are placed out of line, and provenance back to the tree is a separate side table. That is fine, because the safepoint does not need the bijection. The parking transitions are transitions of the \"\n   255\t           [:em \"resolved instruction stream\"]\n   256\t           \" under the execution contract, and a pair \"\n   257\t           [:code \"(segment-hash, pc)\"]\n   258\t           \" names one of them exactly. Naming the AST node instead would mean naming a point that may correspond to several instructions, only some of which park. The owner's ruling that the canonical code form is the positional vector, not entity-attribute-value, was made for code identity and execution order; the safepoints into that code inherit it. Cross-VM safepoints stay at the semantic VM level. There is one fiber, the canonical CESK, and a VM is a coordinate chart on it, not a different bundle.\"]\n   259\t          [:h3 \"Narrowing: the Problem Is the Values\"]\n   260\t          [:p\n   261\t           \"With code identity settled, what is actually not canonical across machines? Control is solved: the segment hash plus the contract stamp fix both which instructions and what they mean. The \"\n   262\t           [:em \"structure\"]\n   263\t           \" of the continuation is solved: a frame is a canonical shape, a return pc with a stack base and a captured environment, and every conforming engine must produce that shape. What a native VM represents in its own domain is every component that holds a \"\n   264\t           [:em \"value\"]\n   265\t           \": the environment, the store, the operand stack, and transitively the environments captured inside frames.\"]\n   266\t          [:p\n   267\t           \"A note on the operand stack, since the classic CESK machine has no such thing. In Felleisen's formulation the continuation \"\n   268\t           [:em \"is\"]\n   269\t           \" the stack: a list of frames, each remembering what to do with the next value, with already-evaluated arguments held inside the frames. Linearization flattens the AST into push and call instructions, and the values that the AST-walking machine kept inside frames move onto a flat vector the semantic VM calls \"\n   270\t           [:code \"St\"]\n   271\t           \". An ordinary return frame then keeps a stack base marking its slice of that shared vector rather than holding the values itself; an effect frame may additionally snapshot the stack it needs. Operationally, then, \"\n   272\t           [:code \"St\"]\n   273\t           \" is where linearization put the work an AST walker holds in its application continuations, not an addition to CESK; the semantic VM files it under control because it is part of what the machine is doing, not part of what it has bound. For transport it adds no new problem; it is simply the place a compiling VM is most likely to have put a value in a register.\"]\n   274\t          [:p\n   275\t           \"So the cross-VM problem is narrow: normalize the value-holding components, and separately make sure a compiled engine can produce them at a safepoint at all. The second is a deoptimization obligation in the JIT sense, and we return to it. The first is the question of a normal form.\"]\n   276\t          [:h3 \"Does CBOR Normalize Them?\"]\n   277\t          [:p\n   278\t           \"The tempting answer is that the wire codec already does. DaoJing stores canonical CBOR: every host produces the same bytes for the same integers of any width, the same float64, the same strings, keywords, vectors, sets, and maps. Three VMs already agree at this layer, since the JVM, Node, and Dart ports share one Jing. Is that the normalization?\"]\n   279\t          [:p\n   280\t           \"It is one of three layers, and the bottom one. CBOR's data model is trees of plain data. The environment, store, and operand stack are not trees of plain data; they are a graph of machine state with identity, with references into the machine, and with references out of it. Each thing in that gap is something no codec can express, and the specification's value grammar is the list of them: a closure points at code and at an environment, so it carries a content hash and an entry pc rather than a tempid; the configuration is a DAG, so sharing is made explicit in a value table rather than duplicated; two cursor references at one position may be independently advanceable, so identity is carried as an emitter-minted cell that is deliberately \"\n   281\t           [:em \"not\"]\n   282\t           \" content-addressed; a cycle is refused; a host function becomes a name resolved under a profile; a stream handle becomes a descriptor the exporter must be able to serve; anything else is refused by path. CBOR carries all of these as ordinary maps and knows nothing of what they mean, and its agreement across hosts holds for Jing's admitted portable domain, with host-capability refusals at its edge. Above both layers sits a third: whether the receiving VM \"\n   283\t           [:em \"computes\"]\n   284\t           \" on the decoded values the way yin does. A sixty-four bit integer that stays exact, a map key equality that agrees, a negated zero that keeps its sign. Bytes and reference shape do not establish that. The grammar's primitive rule reaches toward it, by checking semantic profiles so that only compatible bindings resolve at both ends; what proves it is behavioral parity, running the same programs to the same safepoints on both engines and comparing.\"]\n   285\t          [:h3 \"The Decision: the Transported State Is a Tuple Set\"]\n   286\t          [:p\n   287\t           \"The value grammar's seven rules were written for a nested wire form, markers inside markers. The observation that changed the design was that if the transported state is represented as \"\n   288\t           [:em \"tuples\"]\n   289\t           \", most of those rules stop being special cases. In a tuple store a value is an entity, and two tuples that reference the same entity \"\n   290\t           [:em \"are\"]\n   291\t           \" sharing; the value table disappears. If value positions hold only scalars and entity references, with a program's own map literal becoming an entity of its own, then there is no map at any value position and the marker-forgery problem disappears by construction, under an explicit schema that fixes entity kinds, valid attributes, and reference completeness. A closure is an entity with a segment hash, an entry pc, and a reference to its environment entity. Cycles are the one rule that does not move: tuples can express a reference back to an earlier entity, but the canonical value domain still excludes cycles, because the reference machine cannot produce one and because an immutable cycle has no well-defined per-entity content address. Cycle refusal stays; admitting cycles would be a separate ruling on semantics, addressing, and lowering, and it is deferred. And lowering becomes loading: the tuple set is read through a validating loader into an isolated task, with its program store, its private resource table, its module stores, and its control and scheduler state reconstructed as distinct components, in the same spirit as the code loader's projection path.\"]\n   292\t          [:p\n   293\t           \"What tuples do not dissolve is exactly the residue that was always the real content of the grammar. An entity id is a tempid, local to one machine, the same problem as \"\n   294\t           [:code \":segment 123\"]\n   295\t           \". For an immutable value the entity is content-addressed and deduplication is free. For a cursor cell it must not be, because two cells at one position are distinct, so the id travels as a tempid and is remapped on lowering. This is the specification's separation of content identity from occurrence identity, pushed down to every entity: \"\n   296\t           [:strong \"each entity is either content, hash-addressed and shareable, or identity, carried as a tempid and remapped, and the schema says which.\"]\n   297\t           \" That is the identity discipline, and it is necessary rather than complete. Aliasing survives only under an allocation rule: every source cell gets one transport identity, every reference to it reuses that identity, independent cells keep distinct identities, and lowering allocates exactly one private cell per identity. Content hashing must respect those identities, so two immutable values wrapping two independent cells at the same position never deduplicate into one. Around the discipline the rest of the grammar stays: a disjoint scalar-or-reference value grammar, schema validation of correctly hashed input, reachability checks, and the authentication of resource references before lift and their re-sealing on lower. Tuples remove marker ambiguity; they do not remove forged capabilities, and the two should not be confused. References out of the machine are unchanged: a primitive is still a name under a profile, a stream is still a descriptor, a host object is still refused. Totality is unchanged: the lift either produces the full tuple set or refuses naming the entity and attribute. And equality needs defining: Jing hashes the bytes of whatever value it is given and sorts map keys and set elements, but it preserves vector order and knows nothing of entity references or tempid naming, so the transport schema must fix a canonical representation of the tuple set and the identity renaming under which two sets count as equal. Jing then supplies deterministic bytes and a hash.\"]\n   298\t          [:p\n   299\t           \"This is consistent with the ruling against entity-attribute-value for code. Code was fixed as a positional vector because sequence is its content and a vector resolves execution order for free. State has no order problem; its problem is identity and sharing, which tuples with entity references were built for. Frames and the operand stack, which are sequences, stay positional inside the state, exactly as instructions stay positional inside a segment.\"]\n   300\t          [:h3 \"The Boundary: Tuples Travel, Execution Is Native\"]\n   301\t          [:p\n   302\t           \"The last decision fixes what the tuple set is for. It is the \"\n   303\t           [:em \"transport form only\"]\n   304\t           \". It exists between a lift and a lower. At rest in the tuple space it is queryable by the ordinary query interpreter, so \\\"which parked continuations close over this stream\\\" is a Datalog question rather than a decoder walk, and that is a real capability. But nothing executes it. On arrival the destination VM lowers the tuple set into whatever representation makes execution native there: yin.vm's image, a Wasm linear memory, a Dart isolate's heap. The hot loop runs in that native representation and never queries the transport relation; transients are one way to build it, not a requirement. An earlier design note on the CESK machine in the tuple space proposed, speculatively, that the transition function itself be a Datalog query over the index, and later in the same note proposed the persistence-boundary model, where state is flushed only on park, migrate, or checkpoint. This decision supersedes the first proposal and follows the second. The queryable form is the one at rest.\"]\n   305\t          [:p\n   306\t           \"In the geometry of Part Two this is the cleanest statement yet. The native representation is the fiber over each point. The tuple set is the point-independent representative that transport actually carries. The connection is the pair of maps between them. A foreign VM inherits the whole UCF contract as it stands: the envelope, the dependency closure with its pending waits, dynamic reasons, scheduler and fresh-name state, referenced parked records and module stores, profile satisfaction, total refusal of malformed input, and preservation of the next observable step and the effect trace. What it owes \"\n   307\t           [:em \"in addition\"]\n   308\t           \", for its own representation, is three things:\"]\n   309\t          [:ul.bulleted\n   310\t           [:li\n   311\t            [:strong \"Lift.\"]\n   312\t            \" At a safepoint, project native state to the tuple set, or refuse naming the entity and attribute. Content entities hash; identity entities carry tempids; outward references become names under a profile, stream descriptors, or refusals.\"]\n   313\t           [:li\n   314\t            [:strong \"Lower.\"]\n   315\t            \" Load a tuple set into native state through the destination's own loader, into an isolated task, with identity entities remapped to fresh native ids; or answer profile mismatch, or non-portable for this engine.\"]\n   316\t           [:li\n   317\t            [:strong \"Parity.\"]\n   318\t            \" Two laws, stated separately because they are different kinds of equality. Canonical round trip: lift after lower of a canonical tuple set gives it back, the direction the specification already states for foreign-engine frames, with identity-bearing sets compared under an explicit alias-preserving renaming of ids. Native observational equivalence: lower after lift on one engine need not reproduce the literal native state, since fresh ids and seals differ, but it must preserve the next observable step, the pending waits, the result, and the effect trace. Then the cross-engine row: running the corpus to each safepoint on engine B yields the same canonical tuple set the reference machine yields. That row is the one that catches a VM whose integers overflow or whose \"\n   319\t            [:code \"+\"]\n   320\t            \" disagrees; format cannot supply it, and the profile check only narrows which bindings may be tried.\"]]\n   321\t          [:p\n   322\t           \"Two obligations remain outside the value question and should not be folded into it. Reconstruction: a compiling engine must keep enough at every safepoint to rebuild the environment, operand stack, and frames, which is deoptimization metadata in the JIT sense, and it is what makes lift total or partial for that engine. Full coverage, every canonical safepoint a native safepoint with total reconstruction there, is not a new demand: the specification's foreign-engine contract already requires identical canonical safepoint sets, effect-producing calls included. It is the right demand for a compiled backend generated from the canonical vector, and it is scoped to such programs; it does not claim that an arbitrary Dart isolate or Wasm runtime stack is inspectable or resumable, and a destination that cannot meet a required capability refuses, which is hygiene working, not failing. And effects: a native engine's interaction with its host goes through stream effects like any other, and a protected commitment inherits the complete custody contract of Part Two, the fenced writer with occurrence, epoch, and operation id, the enrolled consumer, atomic admission, intent comparison, durable dedup, and replay or fail-stop. Fencing remains opt-in per stream, and fork compositions remain valid. The custody layer does not move at all, because it lives in the tuple space above every engine and never asks which one holds the grant.\"]\n   323\t          [:h3 \"Why This Is the Hygiene Move Again\"]\n   324\t          [:p\n   325\t           \"The path from the first proposal to the decision is the same lesson Part One drew, learned a third time. The AST-level proposal was a renaming scheme: give every reference a second name in a different coordinate system. Hygiene does not work by adding names; it works by making each reference carry the context that gives it meaning, and the canonical vector already does that. The CBOR proposal was the textual macro's mistake: treat the serialized form as the meaning. The tuple decision is syntax objects for state: every entity is a value plus the mark that says whether it is content or identity, and every outward reference is a name plus the profile that binds it. Changing the carrier from nested maps to tuples does not change what must be carried; it only removes the cases where the old carrier had to say the same thing three different ways.\"]\n   326\t          [:p\n   327\t           \"Status, so the essay does not get ahead of the specification: the decisions in this part are design, not landed code. The nested value grammar is what the current implementation ships and tests. The tuple form of the transported state, its schema, and the conformance harness for a second engine are the next specification change, and they reopen sections with tests behind them.\"]\n   328\t          [:h2 \"The Lineage\"]\n   329\t          [:p\n   330\t           \"None of this is new; all of it is newly joined. Plan 9 taught that a resource is a name in a namespace and that the namespace is per process, so the same path can mean different files to different processes without conflict. UCF's isolated store is that lesson: the resumed task gets its own namespace, and no ambient binding leaks in. Datomic taught that a fact is immutable and that identity is a value computed over content, so two writers who derive the same fact converge. UCF's \"\n   331\t           [:code \":yin.k/id\"]\n   332\t           \" is that lesson, with the one necessary refinement that content identity and occurrence identity must be kept apart. Lisp taught, through forty years of macro pain, that a name is not a meaning until it carries its binding context. UCF's primitive markers, contract stamps, and content hashes are that lesson applied to a runtime instead of a reader. Just-in-time compilers taught that compiled code can be abandoned mid-flight and the interpreter's frame rebuilt from metadata kept at safepoints, and that the reverse, on-stack replacement, enters compiled code at those same points. Part Three's lift is deoptimization across a machine boundary, and its lower is analogous to on-stack replacement, though it may simply start a fresh native task from the canonical frame rather than enter a running activation; the tuple set is the interpreter frame they meet at.\"]\n   333\t          [:p\n   334\t           \"And differential geometry taught that moving a thing without distortion is not a property of the thing but of the connection you move it with, and that on a curved space the best connection in the world still leaves holonomy around loops which you must measure rather than wish away. UCF's seventh section is that lesson, written in datoms.\"]\n   335\t          [:p\n   336\t           \"A continuation is a value, not a session. That is the stance at the head of the UCF specification, and everything else follows from taking it seriously. A value can be content-addressed, so it can be deduplicated and verified. A value can be carried by anything, so no carrier is privileged. A value can be read by anyone, so custody must be arbitrated above the carrier by a boundary that actually possesses something. Hygiene makes the value mean the same thing everywhere. The lease makes sure it runs in only one place at a time. Between them, a suspended computation becomes a vector in a bundle over the distributed world, and UCF is the connection that lets it travel.\"]\n   337\t          [:p [:strong \"Learn more:\"]]\n   338\t          [:ul.bulleted\n   339\t           [:li\n   340\t            [:a\n   341\t             {:href \"/blog/continuations-universal-semantic-kernel.blog\"}\n   342\t             \"Why Continuations Are the Universal Semantic Kernel\"]\n   343\t            \" (why the CESK configuration is the right object to transport in the first place)\"]\n   344\t           [:li\n   345\t            [:a\n   346\t             {:href \"/blog/computation-moves-data-stays.blog\"}\n   347\t             \"Computation Moves, Data Stays\"]\n   348\t            \" (the architectural stance that makes migration the default rather than the exception)\"]\n   349\t           [:li\n   350\t            [:a\n   351\t             {:href \"/blog/beyond-ffi-yin-vm-datom-streaming.blog\"}\n   352\t             \"Beyond FFI: Yin.vm and Datom Streaming\"]\n   353\t            \" (how streams carry opaque values, and why that is the carrier role)\"]\n   354\t           [:li\n   355\t            [:a {:href \"/dao-space.chp\"} \"DaoSpace\"]\n   356\t            \" (the tuple space where the arbitration ledger lives)\"]\n   357\t           [:li\n   358\t            [:a {:href \"/yin.chp\"} \"Yin VM Documentation\"]\n   359\t            \" (the canonical CESK and the semantic contract that UCF stamps)\"]]]]]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
